Problems found
==============

1. SQL injection (critical)  
   • The user-supplied string q is concatenated directly into the SQL text.  
   • Payload: /api/search?q=%27%20OR%201=1-- returns every row; /api/search?q=%27;DROP%20TABLE%20products;-- could destroy data if multiple statements are allowed.  

2. Unhandled errors / wrong control-flow  
   • Most Node DB drivers (mysql2, pg, etc.) are async and return either a Promise or use a callback. The function is not awaited or given a callback, so an unhandled rejection or thrown error will crash the process and no response is sent.  

3. No input validation or length limiting  
   • Empty or 10-MB long q values are accepted ⇒ DoS.  
   • Non-string types (e.g., array) are not rejected.  

4. Missing pagination / row limiting  
   • A search that matches thousands of rows will read them all into memory and send them over the wire.  

5. Potential over-exposure of data  
   • SELECT * returns every column—including ones that might be sensitive (cost_price, supplier_id, etc.).  

6. Results may leak DB internals on error  
   • If db.query throws, Express will dump the error stack to the client unless an error handler is installed.  

7. Performance  
   • LIKE '%term%' prevents use of an ordinary index.  

8. Minor: route does not return 400 when q is omitted.

How to fix / improve
====================

Example with mysql2/promise—adapt as needed for pg, knex, Sequelize, etc.

```js
// Assume you have a centralized error handler: app.use(errorHandler)
app.get('/api/search', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    // 1. Validate input
    const q = String(req.query.q || '').trim();
    if (!q) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Missing search term' });
    }
    if (q.length > 100) {               // arbitrary max length
      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Query too long' });
    }

    // 2. Build parameterised query (prevents SQL-i)
    //    LIKE ? uses one placeholder; the driver escapes wildcards & quotes
    const like = `%${q}%`;

    // 3. Pagination (simple example)
    const limit  = Math.min(Number(req.query.limit)  || 20, 100);
    const offset = Math.max(Number(req.query.offset) || 0 , 0);

    const sql = `
      SELECT id, name, price           -- avoid SELECT *
      FROM   products
      WHERE  name LIKE ?
      LIMIT  ? OFFSET ?;
    `;

    const [rows] = await db.execute(sql, [like, limit, offset]);

    // 4. Return JSON
    res.json(rows);
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);                          // central error handler ⇢ 500
  }
});
```

Extra hardening / performance ideas
-----------------------------------

• Use FULLTEXT indexes or a dedicated search engine (Elastic, Meili, etc.) instead of LIKE '%…%'.  
• Escape % and _ in the user text if you want literal matching (“ESCAPE '\\'”).  
• Rate-limit or throttle the endpoint.  
• Cache popular searches.  
• Log search terms for monitoring/abuse detection.  
• Use helmet, cors, etc., to set HTTP headers.

Summary
-------

The original code is vulnerable to SQL injection, can crash on errors, has no validation, can be abused for DoS, and exposes excess data. Replace string concatenation with parameterised queries, validate/limit input, add pagination, return only necessary columns, and handle errors properly.
